  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nIt is hard to imagine a better late November day with\ntemperatures in the mid to upper 30s under mostly sunny skies \nwith light northeasterly winds, with similar weather expected \ntomorrow. There are a few upper level clouds over mainly western\nWI stemming from a weak upper level wave moving across the \ncentral plains, with some lower level clouds over northwestern \nMinnesota that briefly gave us some broken cloud cover over \nAlexandria. We are about as warm as we will get this afternoon \nwith low temperatures tonight falling into the upper teens \noutside of the Twin Cities and near 20 within the metro core as \nmostly clear skies allow us to efficiently cool alongside \nrelatively weak winds. \n\nThe Thanksgiving holiday tomorrow looks pleasant with highs near\n40 amidst mostly sunny skies for most, with a bit of cloud \ncover filtering mainly over western Wisconsin once again. \nGeneral subsidence will keep winds light and skies clear through\ntomorrow night as lows once again drop into the lower 20s to \nupper teens ahead of our main chance for sensible weather of the\nperiod arriving Friday afternoon in western Minnesota and \nprogressing eastwards along a weak cold front into Saturday \nmorning. A surface low will move west to east over central \nCanada, stretching a weak frontal boundary which will push out \nthe high pressure and subsidence from the previous couple days \nand allow for chances for showers to arrive. Height falls are \nrelatively meager and deep layer forcing is largely absent, so \nmost of the forcing will be tied to the surface to low level \nfront as it moves through. Right now the timing looks to favor \nshowers starting around midday in western MN and ending by \nsunrise Saturday in western WI, with the timing of shower \nchances as they move through being the key to what type of \nprecipitation you can expect. Those that see showers moving \nthrough earlier will experience the warmer part of the system \nand primarily light rain showers or drizzle, while those who do \nnot see precipitation until Friday night into Saturday morning \ncould see some flakes mixing in with the light rain as \ntemperatures fall to around freezing. QPF is extremely low such \nthat we are not expecting any accumulations beyond a hundredth \nof an inch of liquid, so even those that do see snow are not \nexpected to see any impacts, with snow mainly possible over \nwestern Wisconsin. A secondary boundary will move through \nSaturday night into early Sunday, bringing another chance for \nlight rain showers with a bit of snow possibly mixing in to \nsouthern MN and western WI, once again with little in the way of\naccumulation.\n\nUpper level flow becomes zonal as we start next week with a lack\nof strong synoptic scale weather systems to force any \nsignificant weather. A wave or two could produce some cloud \ncover and minor chances for showers, but confidence is too low \nto include anything specific for now. Temperatures wise we trend\non the warmer side with highs in the 40s and lows near the \nfreezing mark through most of next week.",
